zac:5750_lot_33_title rdfs:label "MASOLINO DA PANICALE  (ATTRIBUTED TO) 1383-1440- MADONNA AND CHILD" .

zac:5750_lot_34 a la:Set ;
    rdfs:label "BERNARDINO DEI CONTI. Born at Pavia, end of the XV Century. A follower of Foppa and Civerchio and an imitator of Leonardo da Vinci. Distinguished as a painter of portraits,of which some ten have survived. Died the beginning of the XVI Century. PORTRAIT OF A MAN n an interior is portrayed a young man, half-length, richly dressed, his head turned to the right. He holds in his right hand an open letter and a lute in his left. He looks towards a window, on the sill of which is an inkstand and a leather case. On the wall of the room, in the left-hand top corner, is written:\"Si comel vitiosol vita dispera\" and under the window:\"Sol virtu dom—na morte orr—ida e acerba.\" A third inscription, in the bottom right-hand corner, is evidently more of less modern, but probably replaces the original one, which should be as follows:\"Bernardinusde LuvinusPinxit—1497.\"On the supposition that this inscription comes from the original (perhaps the same, with the exception of Comitis instead of Luvinus), the date could be fixed for the picture; in fact the otlier portraits by this painter bear dates close to this one." ;
    crm:P102_has_title zac:5750_lot_34_title ;
    crm:P1_is_identified_by zac:5750_lot_34_id ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by zac:5750_lot_34_description ;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by zac:creation_5750_lot_34 .

zac:5750_lot_34_description a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label """## 34.PORTRAIT OF A MAN In an interior is portrayed a young man, half-length, richly dressed, his head turned to the right. He holds in his right hand an open letter and a lute in his left. He looks towards a window, on the sill of which is an inkstand and a leather case. On the wall of the room, in the left-hand top corner, is written:"Si comel vitiosol vita dispera" and under the window:"Sol virtu dom—na morte orr—ida e acerba." A third inscription, in the bottom right-hand corner, is evidently more of less modern, but probably replaces the original one, which should be as follows:"Bernardinusde LuvinusPinxit—1497."On the supposition that this inscription comes from the original (perhaps the same, with the exception of Comitis instead of Luvinus), the date could be fixed for the picture; in fact the otlier portraits by this painter bear dates close to this one.\r
\r
Panel:Height,28 inches; width,22 inches.\r
\r
See: A. Venturi, "La Galleria Crespi in Milano, Milan, Hoepli, 1900, pages 255-257; G. Morelli,"Della Pittura Italiana, etc. page 96; B. Berenson,"North Italian Painters of the Renaissance,page I98; J. A. Crowe and G. B. Cavalcaselle,"A History of Painting in North Italy, vol. 2, page 395; U. Thieme, "Allegemeines Lexicon," vol. VII, page 232.\r
##""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416 .

zac:5750_lot_34_id a crm:E42_Identifier ;
    rdfs:label "BO0624_5750-34" .

zac:5750_lot_34_title rdfs:label "BERNARDINO DEI CONTI. Born at Pavia, end of the XV Century. A follower of Foppa and Civerchio and an imitator of Leonardo da Vinci. Distinguished as a painter of portraits,of which some ten have survived. Died the beginning of the XVI Century. PORTRAIT OF A MAN n an interior is portrayed a young man, half-length, richly dressed, his head turned to the right. He holds in his right hand an open letter and a lute in his left. He looks towards a window, on the sill of which is an inkstand and a leather case. On the wall of the room, in the left-hand top corner, is written:\"Si comel vitiosol vita dispera\" and under the window:\"Sol virtu dom—na morte orr—ida e acerba.\" A third inscription, in the bottom right-hand corner, is evidently more of less modern, but probably replaces the original one, which should be as follows:\"Bernardinusde LuvinusPinxit—1497.\"On the supposition that this inscription comes from the original (perhaps the same, with the exception of Comitis instead of Luvinus), the date could be fixed for the picture; in fact the otlier portraits by this painter bear dates close to this one." .

zac:5750_lot_35 a la:Set ;
    rdfs:label "GIULIO CAMPI  Worked principally at Cremona, where he was born, 1500, and died 1572. He emulated the Venetian painters chiefly. THE FLIGHT INTO EGYPT" ;
    crm:P102_has_title zac:5750_lot_35_title ;
    crm:P1_is_identified_by zac:5750_lot_35_id ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by zac:5750_lot_35_description ;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by zac:creation_5750_lot_35 .

zac:5750_lot_35_description a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label """## 35.THE FLIGHT INTO EGYPT\r
\r
The Madonna, seated upon the ass, is much concerned with the Child, Who clings lovingly to her breast, while the angel who precedes them turns his head towards the miraculous apparition of two angels emerging from the trees to offer fruits to the Child. St. Joseph, too, who is following, is arrested by the vision. In the distant landscape, to the left, are seen arches and ruins.\r
\r
Height, 30 inches; width,24 inches.\r
\r
Note:Avery beautiful painting,with extraordinary lighting effects,which led to itshaving been formerly attributed (from the point of view of excellence, not of style) to Lorenzo Lotto. Well preserved.\r
\r
See: Marcel Nicolle,"La Galerie Crespi" Catalogue, ed. Georges Petit,Paris,1914, page 17;B.Berenson,"North Italian Painters of the Renaissance, page 187; Engraving by Cornelis Cort, 1571; Photograph by Anderson, No.3491.\r
##""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416 .

zac:5750_lot_35_id a crm:E42_Identifier ;
    rdfs:label "BO0624_5750-35" .

zac:5750_lot_35_title rdfs:label "GIULIO CAMPI  Worked principally at Cremona, where he was born, 1500, and died 1572. He emulated the Venetian painters chiefly. THE FLIGHT INTO EGYPT" .

zac:5750_lot_36 a la:Set ;
    rdfs:label "ROGIER VAN DER WEYDEN  Born 1397-1400 at Tournay; died at Brussels 1473.  Pupil,or rather co-worker with Robert Campin. He visited Italy about 1449. THE HOLY FAMILY IN A LANDSGAPE;THE DONOR IN ADORATION ACCOMPANIED AND" ;
    crm:P102_has_title zac:5750_lot_36_title ;
    crm:P1_is_identified_by zac:5750_lot_36_id ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by zac:5750_lot_36_description ;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by zac:creation_5750_lot_36 .

zac:5750_lot_36_description a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label """36. THE HOLY FAMILY IN A LANDSGAPE;THE DONOR IN ADORATION ACCOMPANIED AND\r
\r
PRESENTED BY HIS PATRON SAINT,ST.PAUL\r
\r
The holy Virgin, dressed in a gown of soft scarlet, holds the nude Child on her knees; He is embracing a large wooden cross, which The donor is forcefully depicted; he wears a robe of very dark green, edged with narrow gray fur, and in his clasped hands he holds a pair of red gloves, the sign of his nobility. St. Paul is wrapped in a large violet cloak. Behind is the Mother of God, and to her left. St. Joseph sits, asleep, dressed in pale blue and mauve garments. This inactive figure belongs almost to the background, remarkable for its quiet and very interesting mountain scenery. The arrangement directs the attention of the spectator mystically toward the main figures on both sides of the cross. Diagonal old parquetry panel; interesting old frame. (The canvas has been restored at an early date, and would be greatly improved by further restorations.)\r
\r
Oak panel:Height,23 inches; width,181/ inches.\r
\r
The Collection Guicciardi, Milan.\r
\r
See: A. Venturi, "La Galleria Crespi" page 289-29o (reproduction);Baedeker,"Northern Italy"(1go8),page 103; Hoogewerff,"Mededeelingen van het Nederl. Histor. Instituut te Rome, II (1923), page 132 (with reproduction); Friedlander, original of about 146o,with reproduction;Marcelle Nicolle, "La Galerie Crespi, Catalogue, ed. Georges Petit,Paris, 1914, No.96, with reproduction.\r
##""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416 .

zac:5750_lot_36_id a crm:E42_Identifier ;
    rdfs:label "BO0624_5750-36" .

zac:5750_lot_36_title rdfs:label "ROGIER VAN DER WEYDEN  Born 1397-1400 at Tournay; died at Brussels 1473.  Pupil,or rather co-worker with Robert Campin. He visited Italy about 1449. THE HOLY FAMILY IN A LANDSGAPE;THE DONOR IN ADORATION ACCOMPANIED AND" .

zac:5750_lot_37 a la:Set ;
    rdfs:label "WORKSHOP OF FRA ANGELICO  (1387——1455). MADONNA AND CHILD 1531" ;
    crm:P102_has_title zac:5750_lot_37_title ;
    crm:P1_is_identified_by zac:5750_lot_37_id ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by zac:5750_lot_37_description .

zac:5750_lot_37_description a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label """## 37.MADONNA AND CHILD\r
\r
A fervent rendering of the Madonna in voluminous robes and ethereal veiling, seated upon a cushion with her head slightly in- clined towards the Infant Christ, Whom she supports with her left hand while in her right she holds a lily. Angels in the bloom of adolescence, amply draped in classical tunics, stand on either side in attitudes of adoration. The coloring and other characteristics are undoubtedly those of Angelico, though the forms, and expression to a certain extent,lack the complete mysticism and sanctity of Fra Angelico. There is no doubt that the painting came from his workshop, and it is believed some of the workmanship is that of his pupil Andrea di Giusto. In carved and gilded tabernacolo frame of theperiod.\r
\r
Panel:Height,32 inches; width,22 inches.\r
##""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416 .

zac:5750_lot_37_id a crm:E42_Identifier ;
    rdfs:label "BO0624_5750-37" .

zac:5750_lot_37_title rdfs:label "WORKSHOP OF FRA ANGELICO  (1387——1455). MADONNA AND CHILD 1531" .

